00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:00.916
Let me go deeper on the surface layer,
because this is where a lot of the magic happens.

00:10:00.916 --> 00:10:06.916
We maintain two OpenSearch indexes,
one for full text search and one for vector search.

00:10:07.556 --> 00:10:15.836
The vector index uses our GPT legal embedding model, which we fine
tuned specifically on Singapore judgments and legislation.

00:10:16.396 --> 00:10:21.156
This is important because general purpose
embeddings don't capture legal nuance well.

00:10:21.636 --> 00:10:25.636
Terms like consideration or relief have very specific meaning in law.

00:10:27.036 --> 00:10:32.516
Our research combines both approaches with a 6040
weighting between vector and full text.

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:40.476
Why? Because semantic search is great for understanding intent,
but in law, extract terminology matters.

00:10:40.836 --> 00:10:45.396
If a lawyer searches for a specific section
of the penal code, you need exact matching.

00:10:46.036 --> 00:10:48.836
The hybrid approach gives us the best of both worlds.

00:10:50.276 --> 00:10:52.676
On top of that, we have a custom reranker.

00:10:53.636 --> 00:10:56.476
This goes beyond basic, relevant scoring.

00:10:57.236 --> 00:11:04.876
It's a covert based model that fuses multiple signals,
reflecting how legal professionals actually assess relevance.

00:11:07.676 --> 00:11:15.076
Now, this slide is arguably the most important one for our use case
in legal AI responsible AI isn't a nice to have feature.

00:11:15.316 --> 00:11:20.916
It is a hard requirement. We use
Amazon Bedrock Guardrails with two key metrics.

00:11:21.436 --> 00:11:26.836
First answer relevancy that the generated
answer actually address what the user asks,

00:11:28.116 --> 00:11:32.876
and second, grounding is the answer
supported by the retrieved source document.

00:11:34.036 --> 00:11:40.236
Why this matters so much? Because legal
professionals need verifiable, citation backed answers,

00:11:40.916 --> 00:11:46.356
and hallucinated case reference could lead a lawyer
to cite a nonexistent precedent in court.

00:11:47.196 --> 00:11:50.156
So hallucinations are simply unacceptable in this domain.

00:11:51.196 --> 00:11:56.396
Our implementation integrates
Bedrock Guardrails directly into the generation pipeline.

00:11:57.036 --> 00:12:02.516
Every answer is scored, and only answers
that pass our threshold are presented to the user.

00:12:03.276 --> 00:12:06.476
If the system isn't confident enough, it tells the user.

00:12:06.516 --> 00:12:09.716
Rather than guessing this, I don't know.

00:12:09.716 --> 00:12:14.836
Capability has been actually one of the biggest
trust builders with our legal community.

00:12:17.316 --> 00:12:21.356
So now we have a production system that lawyers are using every day.

00:12:22.116 --> 00:12:23.916
And that raises a natural question.

00:12:24.676 --> 00:12:31.156
What if we could find and analyze data on how
the solution is being used and delight our users with new features?

00:12:31.156 --> 00:12:38.796
This is a transition from building AI products to building data
driven AI products, and that requires a proper data foundation.

00:12:39.596 --> 00:12:46.116
I'll now hand over the time to Farid to talk about how
we use analytics services on AWS to improve our product.

00:12:46.956 --> 00:12:47.436
Thanks.

00:12:53.636 --> 00:12:54.636
Thank you Alvaro.

00:12:58.636 --> 00:13:06.196
So like any well designed product, the legal search solution that
Alvaro just went through on Lawnet collects usage information such

00:13:06.196 --> 00:13:12.036
as queries submitted and interactions
information which lets us know how it is being used.

00:13:13.196 --> 00:13:17.156
This presents an opportunity to discover where we can do better.

00:13:17.836 --> 00:13:25.476
We built a data foundation using SageMaker Unified Studio
to measure the quality of this data, run analytics on it,

00:13:25.476 --> 00:13:26.876
and iterate on the product.

00:13:30.276 --> 00:13:34.076
Success in any AI project depends
on getting the data foundation right.

00:13:34.236 --> 00:13:38.556
So allow me now to quickly walk you through
Data Foundation for AI on AWS.

00:13:39.956 --> 00:13:43.476
At the base, we have Amazon S3 as our data substrate.

00:13:44.116 --> 00:13:48.396
S3 stores all types of data.
Unstructured objects like legal documents,

00:13:48.676 --> 00:13:54.036
structured tabular data from our analytics
vector data for AI and metadata.

00:13:54.316 --> 00:13:58.076
S3 is the gravity well that everything flows into.

00:13:59.476 --> 00:14:05.356
Above that is the processing layer with four pillars
analytics services like glue, redshift, and Athena.

00:14:05.356 --> 00:14:12.396
For batch and interactive queries, OLTP databases like Aurora,
DynamoDB, and ElastiCache for operational data,

00:14:12.956 --> 00:14:19.956
SageMaker AI for machine learning workloads and streaming
services like Kafka, Flink, and Kinesis for real time data.

00:14:21.396 --> 00:14:24.876
Then we have the catalog layer. This is the connective tissue.

00:14:25.356 --> 00:14:34.956
It provides catalog semantics and memory, so every data
set is discoverable, has business context, and maintains lineage.

00:14:36.076 --> 00:14:39.116
At the top are the data producers and consumers.

00:14:39.116 --> 00:14:45.316
Our applications Bedrock for generative AI,
QuickSight for visualisation, SageMaker for ML and MCP,

00:14:45.316 --> 00:14:47.556
and other services for agent tooling.

00:14:48.836 --> 00:14:55.116
This foundation serves both humans and AI agents
the same governance catalog data that a data analyst

00:14:55.156 --> 00:14:59.276
queries is the data that an AI agent uses to make decisions.

00:14:59.276 --> 00:15:00.000
That's what makes this a true foundation for both humans and AI.

